A||N||3||Bob Englert||Pressure bleeding tool ?||||1||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||12-19-2000||04:04 PM||||Can anyone recommend a brake master cylinder pressure bleed tool that I can use on the Fiero?  I looked at one at the local auto parts store that was supposed to be for the GM plastic reservoir that consisted of 2 cylinders that fit into the cylinder openings at the base of each container.  Unfortunately their was no instructions included and I couldn't figure out how the fluid is pumped into the system with that setup.  Most I have seen consist of a cap that is clamped over the MC.<P>If anyone has a suggested part number and supplier I would be grateful.<P>Thanks, and happy holidays!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000001||lawrence||12-19-2000||04:26 PM||||If you have a pump sprayer, you can easily make a pressure bleeder.  Here's what I did:<P>Get a spare m/c cap from the bone yard.<P>Drill a hole and mount a hose connector to the center of it.  The plastic cap is thin, so you will need to brace it up a bit.  I used a couple large washers and put nuts on the connector threads on each side of the cap.<P>Then I connected the hose from the pump sprayer to the connector.  This sprayer is one of those things you get at a lawn and garden place that has a reservoir for the stuff you are spraying.  It has a pump to pressurize the reservoir, then you squeeze the trigger to spray your stuff.<P>In this case, you don't put anything in the sprayer.  You just pump it up and put the nozzle trigger on (or remove the nozzle and connect directly to the reservoir).  Don't pump it up too much, because the plastic m/c cap will balloon out and probably pop off.<P>Remember that there isn't much fuid in the m/c, so don't go wild with the bleeding.  Do one brake cylinder, then refill the master.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/sts</A>  - Sequential Turn Signals||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-20-2000||06:02 PM||||While you're at it, invest in a set of SpeedBleeders.  Bleeder screws with a one way check valve (are all check valves one way?).  You loosen them just like you would with a "normal" bleeder, but you don't have to tighten them up before you let off of the brake.  Neat idea at about $20/set of 4.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||12-20-2000||06:14 PM||||Monkeyman, where can I get a set of the speedbleeder screws?<P>Thanks...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
